window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][30] = "<p><em>What I'd tell my teammate.</em> &quot;Great for drafting and for finding gaps. For anything we rely on, we verify against a source, and the research stays with real users.&quot; What would change my practice: a tool approved for confidential data and with traceable citations to the underlying sources, in which case I'd still verify the citations.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What is your concrete workflow for using an AI tool on a PRD? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I write the problem, goal and non-goals myself. Then I give the tool my notes to produce a first draft, a list of edge cases and missing sections, and a plain-language rewrite. I check each factual claim against its source, mark AI-drafted sections in the document, and have an engineer and a designer review it, since they will find errors I would not. I do not paste confidential data into a public tool; I use only a tool my company approves for this data. The decision and the evidence stay with people.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you check an AI summary of user research? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I read the raw notes or transcripts for at least a sample and test the summary against them. I pick five key claims in the summary, find the supporting quote for each, and look for what is missing, such as a minority view or a contradiction. If more than one of five claims has no support, I discard the summary and summarize it myself. I keep the raw material and quotes in the PRD's appendix, so that others can check my reading. A summary is a starting point for my own judgment, not a replacement for reading.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c16-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C16-07 · Hard · execution · EX</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> It is the day before launch. QA finds a bug that loses user data for 0.5% of users, with a three-day fix. Leadership wants to ship on time. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Ship and fix it later,&quot; or &quot;delay.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Classifies the severity, sizes the harm and weighs the delay.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Applies a pre-set release rule, considers staging and its limits (staging still exposes some users), identifies alternatives that remove the harm without delay, and communicates clearly with a go or no-go owner.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> Data loss is an S1 under any sensible release rule: it harms users irreversibly. My starting position is no-go for a full launch until it is fixed or contained, and I'd check that the rule agreed in advance says the same (&quot;no open S1&quot;).</p>\n<p><em>Size the harm.</em> With 200,000 users in the first month (invented), 0.5% is 1,000 people losing data. Even a staged release does not make that safe: at 5% exposure, 50 people could lose data, which I would not accept as a planned outcome. The harm is not recoverable by a patch after the fact.</p>\n<p><em>Size the delay.</em> A three-day delay costs something real: say $15,000 a day of expected revenue and a commitment to partners (invented), $45,000 and some reputational cost. That is small beside 1,000 people's data and the trust cost.</p>\n<p><em>Look for ways to contain without delaying.</em> (1) Can the bug be avoided by disabling the feature path that triggers it, via a feature flag, and launching the rest? (2) Can we launch to users who do not hit the affected condition, with a safe fallback? (3) Can the fix be done in less than three days, or the launch moved by one day with partial fixes verified? (4) Can we warn and offer a backup before the risky action? Any option that removes the data-loss path is acceptable; one that merely reduces it is not.</p>\n<p><em>Decision and communication.</em> Recommend: hold the launch for the fix, or launch with the risky path disabled if that is feasible and verified. Tell leadership the facts in five lines: what the bug does, how many users it could hit, the options with cost and date, my recommendation, and the go or no-go owner (me with the engineering lead, with the sponsor informed). Tell partners and support the new date or the reduced scope.</p>\n<p><em>Afterward.</em> Add a regression test for the case, and look at why testing found it late. What would change my mind: evidence that the affected condition is much rarer than assumed or that the data can be recovered automatically.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What alternatives could remove the harm without delaying the launch? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Several, and I would ask engineering for each: turn off the specific feature path that triggers the bug using a feature flag and ship the rest; block the triggering action with a warning until the fix lands; add a client-side backup before the risky operation; or launch only to the group that cannot hit the path. Each must be tested to be safe, because a workaround that leaves a gap is not a solution. If one works, we can keep the date and ship the fix three days later. If none works, the launch moves.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Who decides, and how do you communicate? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The release owner under the rule agreed in advance, which says no open severity-one bug. I bring facts: 0.5% of 200,000 first-month users is about 1,000 people losing data, a staged 5% release would still expose about 50, the fix takes three days, and the options above. I recommend no-go for a full launch unless a containment option is verified. I put the recommendation and the decision in writing, tell support and customers' contacts what is delayed and why, and record the rule that applied, so that the next team has it.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c16-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Ten exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Figures are assumptions unless marked, and the arithmetic was computed.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c16-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C16-01 · Rewrite the problem statement</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Rewrite each as a problem statement with no solution in it, using who, what happens and the cost: (a) &quot;We need a better onboarding.&quot; (b) &quot;Add a dark mode.&quot; (c) &quot;Users want notifications.&quot; (d) &quot;Make checkout faster.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Four requests have arrived in a ticket queue.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Invent plausible figures and mark them <code>(invented)</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The good-versus-bad test (FW-C16-02), test 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) &quot;New users who sign up on a phone abandon the app within the first session 55% of the time (invented); the biggest drop is at the permission prompts and the empty home screen.&quot; (b) &quot;People who use the app in the evening report eye strain and 12% of low-rated reviews mention brightness (invented).&quot; (c) &quot;Users who miss a time-sensitive event (a delivery, a price change) cannot learn of it without opening the app; 30% of support contacts ask for status (invented).&quot; (d) &quot;At checkout, 22% of people who start do not finish, and recordings show most stall on the address form (invented).&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Keep the original line and add the problem under it, so the requester can see their request is understood.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Problem statements invite other solutions, which can unsettle a requester who had one in mind; explain why.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c16-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C16-02 · Write the non-goals</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> For a feature that lets users save search filters, write three non-goals that a reasonable stakeholder might have expected in scope.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A first release.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Four minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C16.2, non-goals.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (1) Notifications when new results match a saved filter (a later release if usage justifies). (2) Sharing saved filters with other people. (3) Syncing saved filters across devices in version 1 (stored on the device only). Each is plausible for a stakeholder to expect; stating them avoids scope creep, and the third also shortens the build.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a fourth, &quot;no editing of a saved filter's name after creation,&quot; if it saves effort and costs little to users.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Too many non-goals can make the feature feel thin; choose those whose absence the first users will accept.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c16-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C16-03 · Split a story and write acceptance criteria</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Split &quot;As a user, I want to manage my notifications&quot; into three stories using different splitting patterns, and write given-when-then criteria for one.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The story is too big to estimate.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C16-03, INVEST.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> By workflow step: (1) turn all notifications off, (2) choose which categories to receive, (3) set quiet hours. Or by user type: (1) a person with one device, (2) a person with several devices. Criteria for (3): <em>Given</em> I have notifications on, <em>when</em> I set quiet hours from 22:00 to 07:00, <em>then</em> no notification sounds or vibrates in that period and they appear in the notification list afterward. <em>Given</em> a time-critical alert marked as urgent, <em>when</em> it arrives in quiet hours, <em>then</em> it still sounds (if I have allowed urgent alerts). <em>Given</em> I change time zones, <em>when</em> quiet hours apply, <em>then</em> they follow my local time.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Split by simple-then-complex: a single global switch first, then categories, then schedules.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Splitting by technical layer (&quot;database,&quot; &quot;interface&quot;) yields stories no user can test.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c16-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C16-04 · Plan with the outside view</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Past projects of a team ran 1.2, 1.3, 1.5, 1.8, 1.1, 2.2 and 1.4 times their estimates. A new project is estimated at 6 weeks. Compute the median and mean ratios, a planning range, and say what you would do if the date is 7 weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The sponsor wants 7 weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C16-04.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Sorted: 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, 1.4, 1.5, 1.8, 2.2; the median is 1.4 and the mean is 10.5 ÷ 7 = 1.5. At 6 weeks: median plan 8.4 weeks, mean plan 9 weeks, and the worst past case (2.2) 13.2 weeks. A date of 7 weeks sits below the median. Options: cut scope so that the estimated work is about 4.5 weeks (4.5 × 1.5 = 6.8), run a spike to narrow the range, and set a checkpoint at week 3.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Commit to 7 weeks only for the core scope and name what slips if the checkpoint shows an overrun.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Using the history is unpopular because it is longer; it is also more often right.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c16-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C16-05 · Technical-debt payback</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A team of 5 engineers works in 2-week sprints (10 person-weeks per sprint). A fragile module costs 12% of capacity in workarounds. Fixing it costs 9 person-weeks. Compute the interest, the payback, the net over 13 sprints and the break-even interest.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The engineering lead asks for the work.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C16-05.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Interest: 12% × 10 = 1.2 person-weeks per sprint. Payback: 9 ÷ 1.2 = 7.5 sprints. Net over 13 sprints: 13 × 1.2 − 9 = 15.6 − 9 = +6.6 person-weeks. Break-even interest: 9 ÷ 13 ÷ 10 = 6.9% of capacity. If the true interest is 5%, the module costs 0.5 per sprint, the net is 6.5 − 9 = −2.5, and the fix does not pay within the 13 sprints (six months). So the decision depends on measuring the interest.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Stage the fix: do the highest-cost third first (3 person-weeks) and measure the interest saved before continuing.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Fixing all debt is as wrong as fixing none; fix where the interest is highest.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c16-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C16-06 · Triage for release</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Ten days before launch there are 2 S1 defects, 7 S2 (4 on the core path) and 20 S3. Two engineers have 12 person-days. S1 fixes take about 2 days each; core-path S2 fixes about 1.5 days each. The rule: no open S1; S2 only with a workaround and an owner. Compute whether the plan fits and what happens if S1 fixes take 3 days.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A go or no-go decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C16.9 and Worked example 5.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> S1: 2 × 2 = 4 person-days. Core-path S2: 4 × 1.5 = 6. Total 10 of 12, leaving 2 person-days (17%). The 3 non-core S2 ship with workarounds, and the 20 S3 go to the backlog. If S1 fixes take 3 days: 2 × 3 + 6 = 12, no buffer at all; any slip breaks the plan. Then: re-estimate after the first S1 fix, and be ready to launch to a small group first, drop the least severe core-path S2 with a workaround, or take two more days.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Ask whether a feature flag can disable the paths with the S2 defects and keep the launch date.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A buffer of 17% is thin; a stage with a guardrail protects against what the plan missed.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c16-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C16-07 · Map the partners</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A new &quot;buy now, pay later&quot; option at checkout is planned. List the partners to involve, what each needs, and when.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A six-month project.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The partner table in C16.7.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Legal and compliance (credit regulation, disclosures, terms; before design is final); privacy and security (credit data, fraud; at design review); finance (loss rates, fees, funding; at the start, since economics decide viability); risk and fraud teams (underwriting rules, limits; early); support (what customers will ask about payments and disputes; weeks before launch); marketing (how it is described, with disclosures; when the plan forms); design and accessibility (clear terms and totals; during design); data science (default rate measurement and experiment plan; before the build); engineering and the payment partner (integration). Late legal or finance involvement is the most expensive; both can end the project.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a pilot with a small group and a licensed partner to learn before the full build.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Involving everyone early slows the start; skipping any of legal, finance or risk can stop the launch.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c16-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C16-08 · Respond to &quot;not feasible&quot;</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> An engineer says, &quot;That can't be done in this release.&quot; Write a response that gets information, and three questions to ask.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A planning meeting.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Four minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C16.4, handling pushback.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Response: &quot;Thanks for flagging it. Help me understand what makes it hard, so I can adjust the plan.&quot; Questions: (1) What is the hardest part, and is there a cheaper way to achieve the same user outcome? (2) What would make it feasible: more time, a smaller scope, a dependency resolved, a technical spike? (3) What would you cut if we had half the time? Then restate the goal and offer scope options. Avoid &quot;just try,&quot; &quot;it's only a small change,&quot; or appeals to the date.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Ask the engineer to propose the version they would be proud to ship in the time available.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Listening takes longer than pushing, and it surfaces constraints you would otherwise meet in week six.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c16-09\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C16-09 · AI tools: yes, with conditions, or no</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> For each task decide whether to use an AI tool, and with what conditions: (a) draft a spec from your bullet points; (b) list edge cases for a feature; (c) summarize ten customer interviews into themes; (d) generate five &quot;user personas&quot; and treat them as your target users; (e) summarize a spreadsheet of customer complaints that contains names and email addresses.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> <code>[as of 2026-10]</code></p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C16-08.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Yes: edit and verify every claim. (b) Yes: a good use; check which cases matter. (c) With conditions: use it to propose themes from transcripts you supply, check each theme against the transcripts, and quote only real statements; the interviews remain the evidence. (d) No as evidence: invented personas are not users; use them, if at all, as prompts for questions to take to real people. (e) Only with an approved tool and with identifiers removed, per company policy.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> For (c), have a teammate independently read three transcripts and compare with the summary.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> The tools save time on drafts and cost trust if unverified claims slip through.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c16-10\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C16-10 · A timed full answer</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Give a 20-minute answer to: &quot;Walk me through how you would take a new 'schedule a send' feature for an email product from idea to launch.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Self-scored; record yourself.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None. Label what you assume.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Use the 20-minute budget in section 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The execution structure, scored against the six behaviors in section 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> An outline of a strong answer. Clarify the goal: fewer late-night emails sent, or more replies at good times. Spec: problem (people write emails when it suits them and the recipient reads them when it suits the recipient), metric (share of sends that are scheduled; reply rate for scheduled sends; guardrail: send failures), non-goals (recurring sends, scheduling from shared inboxes), requirements in priority order, riskiest assumption (people will use it; check logs for late-night sends). Partners: engineering for the scheduler and failure handling, design for the picker and the states, data science for the measurement, privacy for stored drafts, support for &quot;my email did not send.&quot; Estimates as ranges with a spike for the scheduler. Quality bar: an email must never be lost or sent twice (S1), test across time zones and daylight changes. Staged launch with a flag, a rollback and go or no-go criteria.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Start from the press release the user would read and work backwards to requirements.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A longer spec adds precision and loses readers; keep two pages and a decision log.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for three weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: the spec.</em> Use IQ-C16-01, EX-C16-01, EX-C16-02 and EX-C16-03. Write a two-page spec for a feature you use, with the problem, a metric, non-goals and stories, then run the eight tests in FW-C16-02.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: engineering and design partnership.</em> Use IQ-C16-02, IQ-C16-04, IQ-C16-05, EX-C16-04, EX-C16-05 and EX-C16-08. Do the estimate and debt arithmetic by hand and say the conversation aloud.</li>\n<li><em>Week 3: partners, quality and tools.</em> Use IQ-C16-03, IQ-C16-06, IQ-C16-07, EX-C16-06, EX-C16-07, EX-C16-09 and EX-C16-10. Make a go or no-go call with numbers, and run a full 20-minute answer with a partner who raises a conflict midway.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to play the engineering lead who says &quot;that's a quarter&quot; and the designer who says &quot;that will confuse people.&quot; Practice asking for the range, the riskiest part and the criteria, and not defending.</p>\n<p><strong>Review a real spec.</strong> Read a spec from your team or a public design document, and run the eight tests on it. Write down what you would change.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did my spec separate the problem from the solution and state a metric with a guardrail?</li>\n<li>Did I name non-goals a stakeholder might have expected?</li>\n<li>Were requirements testable and in priority order?</li>\n<li>Did I treat estimates as ranges, check the outside view, and negotiate scope?</li>\n<li>Did I involve design, data science and the other partners at the right time?</li>\n<li>Did I handle disagreement with the shared goal, criteria and cheap evidence?</li>\n<li>Did I discuss technical debt with principal, interest and payback?</li>\n<li>Did I set a quality bar, severity rules and a go or no-go owner?</li>\n<li>Did I describe AI tool use with verification and data protection?</li>\n<li>Did I finish with the two-sentence close from section 4?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> For each drill record the date, the prompt, the spec sections you covered, the friction you handled, your score out of ten and the step you rushed.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter on real work over the next three weeks. Each deliverable doubles as an interview story.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Take the last spec your team wrote (or a ticket for an upcoming piece of work) and run the eight tests in FW-C16-02. Rewrite the problem statement and add two non-goals. Share the rewrite with the engineering lead and designer and note what changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> For the next piece of work, write a two-page spec with the skeleton (FW-C16-01) and a decision log. Review it with engineering and design before anyone commits, and keep a list of what they changed.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Ask engineering for a range and the riskiest part on an upcoming estimate. Compare their past estimates with actuals for the last five projects and compute the ratio (the outside view).</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Choose one area of technical debt and run the conversation: measure the interest (hours or incidents), estimate the principal, and compute payback with a sensitivity. Present it next to a feature of similar size.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Draw the partner map for an upcoming launch (C16.7) and book the first conversation with each partner who is not yet involved.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 3.</strong> Set a release rule for the next launch: severity definitions, what must be true to ship, who makes the call. Write it in the definition of done and share it before testing begins.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the rewritten spec (a time you made a team's work clearer), the estimate and debt analyses (a time you worked with engineers using evidence), and the release rule (a time you set a bar before the pressure came). Keep the numbers real, and keep what you did separate from what the team did.</p>\n<span id=\"c16-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c16-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>A good spec separates the problem from the solution, states a metric with a guardrail and explicit non-goals, and lists testable requirements in priority order; its job is alignment, and its length matches the stakes.</li>\n<li>Treat engineering estimates as ranges with assumptions, check them against history, and negotiate scope, not the number; adding people to a short project rarely helps.</li>\n<li>Discuss technical debt as an investment with principal, interest, payback and a sensitivity, compared with feature work, and stage the repayment.</li>\n<li>Settle disagreements among product, design and engineering by restating the shared goal, naming each side's criteria, finding the cheapest evidence and deciding with clear ownership.</li>\n<li>Set the quality bar and severity rules before the pressure, involve legal, security, support and other partners at the right time, and make the release call with the facts; use AI tools for speed with verification, data protection and human ownership of the decision.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c16-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Marty Cagan, <em>Inspired</em> (2nd edition, 2017) and <em>Empowered</em> (2020). On how strong product teams work with engineering and design.</li>\n<li>Mike Cohn, <em>User Stories Applied</em> (2004), and Bill Wake's INVEST checklist <code>[V: Cohn: Addison-Wesley, March 2004, ISBN 9780321205681; Wake: INVEST in Good Stories, and SMART Tasks, XP123, 2003]</code>. On stories and acceptance criteria.</li>\n<li>Frederick P. Brooks, Jr., <em>The Mythical Man-Month</em> (anniversary edition, 1995). On estimates, staffing and communication <code>[V: Brooks, 1975 with editions 1982 and 1995]</code>.</li>\n<li>Colin Bryar and Bill Carr, <em>Working Backwards</em> (2021). Amazon's practice of writing the press release and FAQ first <code>[V: St. Martin's Press, 9 February 2021, ISBN 9781250267597; includes a chapter on managing inputs, not outputs]</code>.</li>\n<li>Betsy Beyer and others (eds.), <em>Site Reliability Engineering</em> (2016). Reliability, release practice and postmortems <code>[V: Site Reliability Engineering, O'Reilly, 2016]</code>.</li>\n<li>Kent Beck with Cynthia Andres, <em>Extreme Programming Explained</em> (2nd edition, 2004). The origins of many agile practices <code>[V: 2nd edition, Addison-Wesley, 16 November 2004, ISBN 9780321278654 (with Cynthia Andres); first edition 1999]</code>.</li>\n<li>US Government Accountability Office, report GAO-14-694 (31 July 2014), testimony GAO-14-824T (31 July 2014), and the security report GAO-14-730 (16 September 2014), all at gao.gov. The primary sources for CS-C16-02.</li>\n<li>Your company's policy on approved AI tools and data handling <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c17\"></span>\n<h2>C17 — Launch, Rollout and Execution Decisions</h2>\n<span id=\"c17-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>&quot;You are launching this next month. Walk me through it.&quot; &quot;The experiment shows a lift but also slower pages. Do you launch?&quot; &quot;Two hours after launch the error rate is up. What now?&quot; &quot;A defect turns up two weeks before manufacturing locks.&quot; These questions ask for judgment at the point where a team commits to the world: what has to be true before you ship, how you expose users to the change, how you decide when results are mixed, and how you act when things go wrong. The interviewer is looking for a PM who treats a launch as a managed risk with criteria set in advance, not as a date.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code> from the round structures candidates commonly report: launch, rollout and rollback scenarios at Google, Meta, Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Netflix, Stripe and others, often as the closing execution question after a design or prioritization answer, and as follow-ups to experiment readouts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. For hardware companies a distinct form appears, where the product cannot be updated after it ships. On the job it is the work of the days before and after a release: readiness reviews, staged ramps, monitoring, decisions under pressure.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> Whether your launch thinking is criteria-based, staged and honest about risk <code>Inferred</code>. Interviewers listen for a definition of readiness that goes beyond &quot;the code is done&quot;; a rollout that limits the blast radius and is sized to detect harm; pre-agreed rollback triggers with thresholds that respect noise; a clear way to decide when results are mixed; an understanding that hardware changes the rules; and a post-launch plan to measure and learn.</p>\n<p>Six observable behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You define launch readiness with criteria and owners, and name the go or no-go decider.</li>\n<li>You choose a rollout strategy (flags, canary, staged ramp) and say how each stage is sized and what advances it.</li>\n<li>You set rollback triggers in advance, with thresholds and windows that account for noise.</li>\n<li>You decide launch or hold from experiment results using a pre-set rule, including partial launches.</li>\n<li>You recognize irreversibility (hardware, public commitments) and adapt evidence, staging and supply planning.</li>\n<li>You plan the post-launch review: what you will measure, when, and what would make you iterate, scale or stop.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Six patterns end launch answers: &quot;we launch when the code is done,&quot; a 100% release on a Friday with no way back, rollback decided in the middle of the incident by whoever is loudest, a ramp too small to detect the harm you worry about, treating hardware like software, and no plan to learn after launch.</p>\n<span id=\"c17-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c17-c17-1-defining-launch-readiness\"></span>\n<h4>C17.1 Defining launch readiness</h4>\n<p>A launch is a state of readiness crossed on purpose, not a date. A team is ready when a short list of criteria is met, each with an owner, and a named person decides go or no-go. FW-C17-01 is the gate.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>The question</th>\n<th>Example criterion</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Product</td>\n<td>Does it meet its acceptance criteria and quality bar (C16.9)?</td>\n<td>No open S1; S2 only with workaround and owner</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reliability</td>\n<td>Will it hold up, and can we undo it?</td>\n<td>Load tested to 2× expected peak; rollback rehearsed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data</td>\n<td>Can we tell whether it worked?</td>\n<td>Events logged and checked; dashboards live; baseline recorded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Support</td>\n<td>Can people get help?</td>\n<td>Scripts and training done; escalation route named</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Communication</td>\n<td>Do those affected know?</td>\n<td>Internal note, customer message, status page ready</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Legal, privacy, security</td>\n<td>Have constraints been met?</td>\n<td>Sign-offs recorded; open risks accepted by a named owner</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Accessibility and localization</td>\n<td>Does it work for everyone intended?</td>\n<td>Accessibility pass done (C10.6); languages complete</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rollout plan</td>\n<td>How will exposure grow?</td>\n<td>Stages, triggers and owners agreed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <strong>go or no-go</strong> decision belongs to one named person (often the PM with the engineering lead), informed by the checklist, with dissent recorded. A meeting where &quot;everyone agrees&quot; and nobody owns the decision is a risk in itself. Readiness is also graded by stakes: a copy change needs a light gate, a payments change needs the full one (C02's decision classifier).</p>\n<p><strong>When not to hold a heavy gate.</strong> For small, reversible changes behind a flag with automatic monitoring, a light gate and a fast rollback beat a long review. Process should scale with the cost of being wrong.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> A team announces &quot;launch is Friday&quot; in week one and treats the date as the goal. Testing finds problems on Thursday; there is no rollback rehearsal; support learns of the change from customers. The date was met and the launch failed. A team that wrote the criteria in week one, assigned owners and had a go or no-go meeting on Wednesday would have found the gaps with time to act, and could have chosen a smaller launch on Friday.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hidden risks.</strong> Two. <em>Checklist theater:</em> every box ticked and nobody asked whether the criteria were the right ones. <em>Deadline gravity:</em> the closer the date, the more the criteria bend. Write the criteria early and agree that only the decider may waive one, in writing.</p>\n<span id=\"c17-c17-2-rollout-strategies-staged-releases-feature-flags-canar\"></span>\n<h4>C17.2 Rollout strategies: staged releases, feature flags, canaries</h4>\n<p>The safest way to launch is to expose a change to a few users first and widen only when it behaves. Four ideas make that possible.</p>\n<p>A <strong>feature flag</strong> is a switch that turns functionality on or off for chosen users without shipping a new release. A <strong>staged rollout</strong> is releasing a change to progressively larger shares of users while monitoring for problems. A <strong>canary release</strong> is a first release to a small subset of users or servers that acts as an early warning for the rest; the name comes from the canary in the coal mine. <strong>Blast radius</strong> is how many users or systems a failure would affect. Staging and flags exist to shrink the blast radius.</p>\n<p><strong>A rollout ladder (FW-C17-02).</strong> A typical ladder: internal use (employees), a canary (1% of users), 10%, 25% or 50%, then 100%, with a minimum dwell time at each stage and a set of conditions to advance: no rollback trigger hit, guardrails within margins, no unexplained anomalies. Each stage has an owner who watches the dashboards, and a defined way back (turn the flag off).</p>\n<p><strong>Size the stages to detect the harm you fear.</strong> A stage that is too small cannot see a modest problem. Take an error rate of 1% (invented). To detect a rise of 0.5 points (to 1.5%) with 80% power at the 5% level, with the rest of the users as the comparison, a stage needs about 4,700 users. To detect a rise of 0.3 points it needs about 11,300; to detect 0.1 points, about 93,400. On a base of 1,000,000 users, a 1% canary (10,000 users) can reliably see a rise of about 0.33 points or more; seeing 0.1 points needs about 9% exposure. So the 1% canary protects against severe failures, and the move to 10% is what protects against subtle ones. Choose stage sizes and dwell times from the smallest harm you are unwilling to accept, using the same arithmetic as in C12 and C14.</p>\n<p><strong>Dwell time.</strong> Some problems appear only with time or scale: a memory leak after hours, a weekly cycle, a rare path. Hold each stage long enough to cover the cycle that matters (at least a business day, and a week for weekly patterns), and watch the lagging indicators (support contacts, refunds) as well as the fast ones.</p>\n<p><strong>Other forms.</strong> <em>Dark launch:</em> run the new code path in production without showing results, to test load and correctness. <em>Geographic staging:</em> start in one market. <em>Opt-in beta:</em> users choose, which gives goodwill and a biased sample. <em>Percentage ramps by cohort:</em> new users first. <em>Kill switch:</em> a flag that turns off a risky component fast.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Staging trades a little speed for a much smaller worst case. <strong>When not to stage.</strong> When the change cannot be partially released (a database migration, a legal change on a date) or when staging itself adds risk; then test harder before, and plan the way back.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Regional Lens: India.</strong> Where one operating system carries most users (C07.10) but phones vary widely, a rollout by percentage matters more. Google Play lets a developer release to a percentage of users and widen it by hand; Apple's phased release for automatic updates runs over seven days to a random sample of users, who can still be reached by a manual download <code>[V: Apple App Store Connect Help, Release a version update in phases; Google Play Console Help as quoted by a how-to guide]</code>. Watch crash rates and payment success by device class and Android version as well as overall, because a failure on low-end phones can be small in the average and large for the people on them <code>Inferred</code>. Read each store's current rules yourself at the time you read this book <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c17-c17-3-hardware-and-device-launches-why-irreversibility-chang\"></span>\n<h4>C17.3 Hardware and device launches: why irreversibility changes rollout, rollback and supply planning</h4>\n<p>Software can be changed after release; most hardware cannot. This single fact changes the logic of a launch.</p>\n<p><strong>What is different.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No simple rollback.</strong> A shipped device is in a customer's hands. The nearest equivalents are a software or firmware update (if the device can receive one and the defect is fixable that way), a repair or replacement program, or a recall, each slow and expensive.</li>\n<li><strong>Lock dates.</strong> Tooling, component orders and manufacturing slots are committed months ahead. After a lock date, changing a physical part means a delay measured in weeks and a cost measured in millions.</li>\n<li><strong>Supply planning.</strong> The first production run is a bet on demand: too few units means lost sales and a launch with shortages; too many means inventory risk. Lead times for components and second runs are long.</li>\n<li><strong>Certification.</strong> Many devices need safety and regulatory approvals before sale, which fix the design <code>[V: FCC equipment authorization, 47 CFR Part 2 and section 2.1043 on firmware (permissive changes); US rules for radio devices only; safety approvals and non-US regimes not checked]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>The update path is part of the product.</strong> A fix-later plan only works if the device can receive the update. If the defect breaks the connection or the first-boot step that updates need, then &quot;we'll fix it in software&quot; is not a plan.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How the approach changes (FW-C17-03).</strong> Classify each decision by reversibility: reversible (a flag), hard to reverse (a public price), irreversible (a manufactured part). Demand more evidence for the irreversible: more testing before the lock (reliability, abuse, environmental, field trials with real users), explicit gating of the lock on test results, staged availability by region or channel, and a validated fix-later path. Quantify the cost of each option, including the cost of a defect in the field (returns, support, replacement, reputation) against the cost of a delay.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision under irreversibility.</strong> When a defect appears before the lock, the options are usually: delay the lock, lock on time with a validated update path for the fix, or lock on time and accept the defect. Worked example 3 sets out the arithmetic and a gating approach: validate the fix path early enough that you can still delay if it fails.</p>\n<p><strong>Safety is different.</strong> A defect that can hurt people is not a tradeoff against revenue. The bar is much higher, and the case in section 10 shows the cost of getting it wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> It shifts effort to before the lock, where changes are cheap, and keeps an escape route. <strong>When not to over-apply it.</strong> To software-only products, where staging and rollback are available and cheaper than extra pre-launch testing.</p>\n<span id=\"c17-c17-4-launch-or-no-launch-given-experiment-results\"></span>\n<h4>C17.4 Launch or no launch, given experiment results</h4>\n<p>Experiment results rarely come out clean. Use the pre-set decision rule from C14.5 (FW-C14-05), and extend it for launch decisions with partial options (FW-C17-04). The possible actions are: <em>launch to everyone</em>, <em>launch in stages with a long-term holdout</em>, <em>extend the test</em> (the interval is too wide), <em>fix and retest</em> (a guardrail breached for a reason you can address), or <em>do not launch</em>.</p>\n<p>Read results in the order of C14.8: validity first (sample ratio, novelty), guardrails next, then the primary metric, then secondary metrics and segments, then the time horizon. Then ask launch-specific questions: what is the cost of being wrong in each direction (a false launch against a missed launch), is the decision reversible (it often is, behind a flag), does the effect last (novelty), and is a long-term holdout in place to check.</p>\n<p>Take an example (invented). The primary metric, purchases, shows +0.4 points with an interval of +0.1 to +0.7, against a smallest worthwhile effect of +0.3. The latency guardrail shows +30 ms with an interval of +10 to +50 against a margin of +50 ms: within the margin, just. The support-contacts guardrail shows +2% with an interval of −1% to +5% against a margin of +3%: the upper end breaches. Under the strict rule from C14.5 the answer is not to launch as is: the primary interval's lower end is below the threshold and a guardrail breaches. A sensible plan: investigate the support contacts (what are they about?), extend the test to narrow the interval (four times the data halves it), and if the cause is a confusing label, fix and retest. The value at stake gives urgency: +0.4 points on 5,000,000 visitors a month at $10 a purchase is about $200,000 a month, so each month of delay has a price, and a staged launch with the guardrails watched is a reasonable alternative if the cause is understood and the rollback is quick. The key is to decide on the rule and the cost of delay, not on impatience.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> The decision follows a rule written before the data, and partial options avoid a false choice between all and nothing. <strong>When not to wait for certainty.</strong> When the change is reversible, the downside is bounded by staging, and the cost of delay is large.</p>\n<span id=\"c17-c17-5-rollback-triggers-and-incident-response-at-pm-level\"></span>\n<h4>C17.5 Rollback triggers and incident response at PM level</h4>\n<p>A <strong>rollback</strong> is reverting a release to its previous state. The most important decision about rollback is made before launch: the conditions under which you will do it. A <strong>rollback trigger</strong> is a pre-agreed condition that, when met, causes a release to be reverted or paused. FW-C17-05 is a sheet: for each trigger, the metric, the threshold, the window, the owner and the action.</p>\n<p><strong>Thresholds must respect noise.</strong> A threshold inside the normal variation fires on chance; one far outside it fires too late. Use the noise band (C13, C12). Take a payment success rate with a baseline of 96% and about 1,000 attempts per half hour (invented). The standard error of the rate over half an hour is √(0.96 × 0.04 ÷ 1,000) = 0.62 points, so a drop of 1.0 point is only 1.6 standard errors: it will happen by chance often. Over two hours, with 4,000 attempts, the standard error is 0.31 points, and a 1.0-point drop is 3.2 standard errors: a reliable signal. So the trigger might read: &quot;Payment success below 95.0% over a rolling two hours, or below 92% over any 15 minutes, which is a large drop that is visible even in a small window.&quot; Pair a sensitive-but-slow trigger with a fast-but-coarse one.</p>\n<p><strong>Who decides.</strong> The trigger names the owner and the action, so that no one has to argue at 2 a.m. Typically the on-call engineer can roll back immediately on a hard trigger; the PM and engineering lead decide on judgment calls.</p>\n<p><strong>Incident response at PM level (FW-C17-06).</strong> In an incident, engineers work the technical problem under an incident lead. The PM's job is different and no less important:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Confirm the impact:</em> who is affected, how many, how badly (C13 step zero).</li>\n<li><em>Contain first:</em> turn off the flag or roll back if a trigger is hit; diagnose after.</li>\n<li><em>Communicate:</em> internal status at a fixed cadence; the customer-facing message; support told what to say (the five-line status from C13.8).</li>\n<li><em>Decide on scope and tradeoffs</em> the team brings to you: roll back versus fix forward, partial disable, customer remediation.</li>\n<li><em>Protect the team:</em> one voice to leaders, fewer interruptions for the people fixing.</li>\n<li><em>Record:</em> a timeline as it happens.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A <strong>postmortem</strong> is a blameless review after an incident, documenting the timeline, causes, what went well and badly, and actions with owners. Blameless means the question is what in the system allowed this, not who is at fault; people then report problems early. The PM writes or co-owns the product-impact and decision sections, and makes sure the actions get done.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Triggers made in calm are better than decisions made in crisis, and a PM who handles communication frees engineers to fix. <strong>When not to roll back.</strong> When the cost of rolling back exceeds the harm (a data migration that cannot be reversed without loss), or when a fast fix forward is clearly safer; say so and decide deliberately.</p>\n<span id=\"c17-c17-6-delivery-risk-dependencies-estimates-scope-cuts\"></span>\n<h4>C17.6 Delivery risk: dependencies, estimates, scope cuts</h4>\n<p>Launches slip because of things outside the team's control: other teams, vendors, approvals, hardware lead times. FW-C17-07 is a map.</p>\n<p><strong>List dependencies and rate them.</strong> For each: what is needed, from whom, by when, the probability it arrives on time, the impact if late, and the mitigation. <strong>Risk compounds.</strong> If a launch needs four independent dependencies and each is 90% likely to be on time, the chance that all four are on time is 0.9⁴ = 66%. With five at 90%, it is 59%. With six at 92%, 61%. A plan that treats each as &quot;probably fine&quot; is a plan that has a one-in-three chance of slipping. Reduce the count of dependencies on the critical path, raise the probability of the weakest, add buffer where they cluster, and start the longest-lead items first. If two of four are raised to 97% by early engagement, the chance rises to 0.9² × 0.97² = 76%.</p>\n<p><strong>Estimates.</strong> Use ranges and the outside view (C16.4), re-estimated at checkpoints. <strong>Scope cuts.</strong> Decide in advance the order in which you would cut if the date is threatened: first nice-to-haves with high effort and low value (C15's value-per-effort), never the launch criteria themselves (quality bar, safety, accessibility). A staged launch is itself a scope cut: launch to fewer users, or with fewer features, on the date, and widen later.</p>\n<p><strong>Track and escalate early.</strong> Review the map weekly; a dependency at risk is raised the week you see it, with options, not the week it fails.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Seeing the compounding turns vague worry into a number, and an ordered cut list turns panic into a plan. <strong>When not to map everything.</strong> For a small change with one or two dependencies, a short note is enough.</p>\n<span id=\"c17-c17-7-pre-launch-checklist\"></span>\n<h4>C17.7 Pre-launch checklist</h4>\n<p>The checklist is the gate (C17.1) made concrete. A compact version, to adapt:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Product and quality:</strong> acceptance criteria met; defects triaged by severity; no open S1; accessibility pass done.</li>\n<li><strong>Reliability:</strong> load test done; monitoring and alerts live; rollback rehearsed; flag off-switch tested.</li>\n<li><strong>Data:</strong> instrumentation verified in production; dashboards and baselines ready; experiment or holdout configured; success criteria written.</li>\n<li><strong>Rollout:</strong> stages, dwell times, ramp criteria and rollback triggers agreed; owners named; on-call arranged (including the PM for decisions).</li>\n<li><strong>People:</strong> support trained and scripts ready; sales and success informed; internal and external messages drafted.</li>\n<li><strong>Constraints:</strong> legal, privacy, security and policy sign-offs recorded; open risks accepted in writing.</li>\n<li><strong>Decision:</strong> go or no-go meeting held; decider named; dissent recorded.</li>\n<li><strong>After:</strong> post-launch review dates set (day 1, week 1, week 4); owners for follow-up.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c17-c17-8-post-launch-measurement-and-iteration\"></span>\n<h4>C17.8 Post-launch measurement and iteration</h4>\n<p>Launch is a beginning. Plan the review before you ship (FW-C17-08). At day one, check health: errors, performance, support contacts, rollback triggers. At week one, check the primary metric against the target, guardrails and early cohorts, and look for novelty (C14.4): an early peak is not the steady state. At week four or later, check retention and the long-term holdout, and decide: <em>iterate</em> (the direction is right, details to improve), <em>scale</em> (invest more), or <em>stop</em> (it did not work, withdraw it and say why). Compare with the success criteria you wrote before launch, not with a new story afterward.</p>\n<p>If a metric regresses after launch, use the diagnosis tools of C13: confirm it is real, locate it, compare with the launch timeline, and decide on rollback or fix. Record what you learned in a short launch review so that the next team begins with it. Include what surprised you; it is where the learning is.</p>\n<span id=\"c17-c17-9-interview-forms\"></span>\n<h4>C17.9 Interview forms</h4>\n<p>Section 4 sets out the answer architecture and the types: walk me through a launch; the metric regressed after launch; launch or hold given results; a hardware defect before a lock date; engineering says three times longer; define success for X; and an incident at 2 a.m.</p>\n<span id=\"c17-c17-10-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h4>C17.10 Worked examples</h4>\n<p>Section 5 gives five worked examples: sizing a rollout ladder, a launch or hold decision with mixed results, a hardware defect before a manufacturing lock, rollback triggers that respect noise, and compounding delivery risk. Section 11 adds seven questions with model answers, including an Apple hardware question and an Amazon launch question.</p>\n<span id=\"c17-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Eight frameworks, set out below. The experiment decision-rule table (FW-C14-05), the conflicting-metrics readout (FW-C14-07), the status-message template (FW-C13-07), the estimate conversation (FW-C16-04), the quality bar and severity rules (C16.9) and the decision classifier (FW-C02-04) are reused, not repeated.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c17-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C17-01 · The launch readiness gate · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a planned launch and its stakes.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a checklist across eight categories (product, reliability, data, support, communication, legal and security, accessibility and localization, rollout) with an owner for each, and a named go or no-go decider.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> criteria are written before the date pressure builds.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a gate costs time and prevents surprises; scale it to the stakes.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a date as the goal; criteria waived silently; no named decider.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the gate in CS-C17-01 and the checklist in C17.7.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"fw-c17-02\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C17-02 · The rollout ladder · Original</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> the change, the user base, the smallest harm you will not accept.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> stages with exposure share, dwell time, advance conditions and an owner; stage sizes chosen to detect the target harm.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> a flag or release mechanism allows partial exposure; the control is the remaining users.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> more stages are safer and slower.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a canary too small to detect the harm; too short a dwell time; no way back.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 1.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Stage</th>\n<th>Exposure</th>\n<th>Dwell</th>\n<th>Advance if</th>\n<th>Owner</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Internal</td>\n<td>Staff</td>\n<td>1 day</td>\n<td>No S1; instrumentation correct</td>\n<td>Eng lead</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Canary</td>\n<td>1%</td>\n<td>1 business day</td>\n<td>No trigger hit; guardrails within margin</td>\n<td>On-call</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Early</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n<td>2 days</td>\n<td>Same; subtle harms ruled out at the planned size</td>\n<td>PM and eng lead</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Broad</td>\n<td>50%</td>\n<td>3 days</td>\n<td>Same; support contacts normal</td>\n<td>PM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Full</td>\n<td>100%</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Long-term holdout kept</td>\n<td>PM</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c17-03\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C17-03 · The irreversibility check · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a decision and the options.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a reversibility class (reversible, hard to reverse, irreversible), the evidence bar it implies, a fix-later path test, and the staging and supply implications.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> reversibility can be judged from the cost and time of undoing.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> extra testing and gates before an irreversible step cost time and prevent costly mistakes.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> treating hardware like software; a fix-later path that depends on the broken part; no gate on the lock.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 3.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Class</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Reversible</td>\n<td>A flagged feature</td>\n<td>Move fast; monitor; roll back</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hard to reverse</td>\n<td>A public price, an announcement</td>\n<td>More evidence; stage; prepare the retraction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Irreversible</td>\n<td>A manufactured part, a safety-critical release</td>\n<td>Highest bar; gate the lock; validate the fix-later path; consider safety first</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c17-04\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C17-04 · The launch-or-hold decision table · Original</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> an experiment readout, the pre-set rule, the cost of delay and the reversibility.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> one of five actions (launch, staged launch with holdout, extend, fix and retest, do not launch) with the reason.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the rule was fixed before the data (FW-C14-05).</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> partial options reduce regret and add complexity.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a binary choice; ignoring the cost of delay; overriding a guardrail silently.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 2 and C17.4.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Readout</th>\n<th>Action</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary clears the threshold; guardrails within margins; no validity issue</td>\n<td>Launch (stage if risk warrants), keep a holdout</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary positive but interval straddles the threshold; guardrails fine</td>\n<td>Staged launch with holdout, or extend</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A guardrail interval breaches its margin</td>\n<td>Investigate; fix and retest; do not launch as is</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary below threshold</td>\n<td>Do not launch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Validity failure</td>\n<td>Rerun</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c17-05\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C17-05 · The rollback trigger sheet · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> the metrics that signal harm and their noise levels.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> for each trigger: metric, threshold, window, owner, action; a fast coarse trigger and a slow sensitive one.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the baseline and noise are known (C13, C12).</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> tight triggers cause false rollbacks; loose ones cause late ones.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> thresholds inside the noise; no owner; deciding triggers during the incident.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 4.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Metric</th>\n<th>Threshold</th>\n<th>Window</th>\n<th>Owner</th>\n<th>Action</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c17-06\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C17-06 · The incident playbook for PMs · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> an alert or a report of harm after launch.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the first 15 minutes: confirm impact, contain, communicate, decide, log; then a blameless postmortem with actions.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> an incident lead exists; the PM's role is impact, decisions and communication.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a fast rollback may hide the cause; keep logs and a reproduction.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> debating cause before containing; silence from the PM; blame in the postmortem.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> IQ-C17-07.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: <strong>Confirm impact → Contain → Communicate (cadence) → Decide tradeoffs → Log → Postmortem (blameless, with owners).</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c17-07\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C17-07 · The dependency and delivery-risk map · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> the launch plan and its dependencies.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a table of dependencies with probability on time, impact, mitigation and start date; the compounded probability; the order of scope cuts.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> dependencies are independent enough for a first estimate.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a map costs an hour a week and avoids late surprises.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> treating each dependency as fine; compounding ignored; raising risks late.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 5.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dependency</th>\n<th>Needed by</th>\n<th>P(on time)</th>\n<th>Impact if late</th>\n<th>Mitigation</th>\n<th>Owner</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n</table>\n<p>Compounded probability: <strong>multiply the probabilities of the independent dependencies on the critical path.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c17-08\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C17-08 · The post-launch review · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> the launch, its success criteria and baselines.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> reviews at day 1, week 1 and week 4: health, metrics against targets, guardrails, novelty check, holdout; a decision to iterate, scale or stop; a written launch review.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> success criteria were written before launch.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> time spent reviewing against shipping the next thing.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> moving the goalposts; reading week one as steady state; no written learning.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the review plan in IQ-C17-04 and EX-C17-09.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c17-4-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>4. Answer architecture</h3>\n<span id=\"c17-the-launch-answer-for-a-25-minute-round\"></span>\n<h4>The launch answer, for a 25-minute round</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Minutes</th>\n<th>What to produce</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Clarify the launch</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>What, who, where, stakes, reversibility; what is fixed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Readiness criteria</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>The gate by category, owners, the go or no-go decider</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rollout plan</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Ladder with stage sizes, dwell times and advance conditions; flags and canary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Triggers and decisions</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Rollback triggers with thresholds and windows; the launch-or-hold rule for mixed results</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risks and dependencies</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>The dependency map and compounding; hardware or irreversible elements; scope-cut order</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Measurement and iteration</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Day 1, week 1, week 4 review; iterate, scale or stop</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slack</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Pacing rules.</strong> If the interviewer gives a specific scenario (a regression, a defect), spend the time on that and use the structure briefly. If you run behind, keep the gate, the ladder and the triggers.</p>\n<p><strong>How to say it.</strong> State the criteria early: &quot;I'd launch when [criteria] are met, decided by [owner], and I'd expose it in stages: 1%, 10%, 50%, 100%, with a rollback if [trigger].&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>How to close.</strong> Two sentences: &quot;I'd define readiness with owners and a named decider, ramp in stages sized to catch the harm I fear, set rollback triggers before launch, and review at day one, week one and week four. I'd change the plan if [signal].&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c17-type-walk-me-through-a-launch\"></span>\n<h4>Type: walk me through a launch</h4>\n<p>Use the full structure.</p>\n<span id=\"c17-type-the-metric-regressed-after-launch\"></span>\n<h4>Type: the metric regressed after launch</h4>\n<p>Use the incident playbook (FW-C17-06) and the diagnosis spine (C13): confirm, contain by trigger, locate, decide, communicate.</p>\n<span id=\"c17-type-launch-or-hold-given-results\"></span>\n<h4>Type: launch or hold given results</h4>\n<p>Use the decision table (FW-C17-04) and the pre-set rule; quantify the cost of delay.</p>\n<span id=\"c17-type-hardware-or-irreversible-launch\"></span>\n<h4>Type: hardware or irreversible launch</h4>\n<p>Use the irreversibility check (FW-C17-03): lock date, fix-later path, supply, certification, safety.</p>\n<span id=\"c17-type-engineering-says-three-times-longer-or-a-slipping-date\"></span>\n<h4>Type: engineering says three times longer, or a slipping date</h4>\n<p>Use the dependency map and scope cuts (C17.6, C16.4, C15.4).</p>\n<span id=\"c17-type-define-success-for-x\"></span>\n<h4>Type: define success for X</h4>\n<p>Use the success-metric template (C05) and the post-launch review.</p>\n<span id=\"c17-common-interviewer-interventions-and-what-to-do\"></span>\n<h4>Common interviewer interventions, and what to do</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Interviewer says</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;When is it ready?&quot;</td>\n<td>Readiness</td>\n<td>Give criteria, owners and the decider, not a date</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Why 1%?&quot;</td>\n<td>Stage sizing</td>\n<td>Show what harm that size can detect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What makes you roll back?&quot;</td>\n<td>Triggers</td>\n<td>Give metric, threshold, window and owner, with noise</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;The test is mixed.&quot;</td>\n<td>Launch or hold</td>\n<td>Apply the pre-set rule; offer a staged option</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Can't you fix it in software later?&quot;</td>\n<td>Hardware</td>\n<td>Test the update path; reversibility; the lock date</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;It's 2 a.m. and errors are up.&quot;</td>\n<td>Incident</td>\n<td>Confirm, contain, communicate, then diagnose</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;How will you know it worked?&quot;</td>\n<td>Measurement</td>\n<td>Day 1, week 1, week 4; criteria set beforehand</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c17-5-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h3>5. Worked examples</h3>\n<p>All five examples are <code>Illustrative</code>: the products are fictional or use a product type as the setting only, and every figure is an invented assumption. The arithmetic was computed. Each is written as notes you could say aloud.</p>\n<span id=\"c17-worked-example-1-sizing-a-rollout-ladder-to-catch-the-harm-y\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 1 — Sizing a rollout ladder to catch the harm you fear (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A web product with 1,000,000 users (invented) is releasing a rewritten account page. The team's main worry is a rise in errors. The error rate is 1% today.</p>\n<p><strong>What each stage can see (FW-C17-02).</strong> Comparing the exposed users with everyone else, 80% power at the 5% level:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Rise in error rate to detect</th>\n<th>Users needed in the stage</th>\n<th>Share of 1,000,000</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>+0.5 points (1.0% to 1.5%)</td>\n<td>about 4,700</td>\n<td>0.5%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>+0.3 points</td>\n<td>about 11,300</td>\n<td>1.1%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>+0.1 points</td>\n<td>about 93,400</td>\n<td>9.3%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A 1% canary (10,000 users) can reliably see a rise of about 0.33 points or more. To see 0.1 points the stage must be about 9% of users. At a 1% error rate, 0.1 points is 1,000 extra failures per million users.</p>\n<p><strong>The ladder.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Stage</th>\n<th>Exposure</th>\n<th>Dwell</th>\n<th>What it can detect</th>\n<th>Advance if</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Internal</td>\n<td>Staff</td>\n<td>1 day</td>\n<td>Gross failures, wrong data</td>\n<td>No S1; events logged correctly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Canary</td>\n<td>1%</td>\n<td>1 business day</td>\n<td>Severe harm (about +0.33 points of errors or more)</td>\n<td>No trigger hit; errors within 0.3 points of baseline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Early</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n<td>2 days</td>\n<td>Subtle harm (+0.1 points) and slow problems (memory growth over hours)</td>\n<td>Errors within 0.1 points; latency within margin; support contacts normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Broad</td>\n<td>50%</td>\n<td>3 days, covering a weekend</td>\n<td>Weekly patterns; rare paths</td>\n<td>Same</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Full</td>\n<td>100%</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>Keep a 2% holdback for two weeks to compare</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Reasoning behind the stages.</strong> The canary protects against a severe failure at a small blast radius: if the page breaks outright, 10,000 users are affected for a day at most, with a flag to turn it off. The move to 10% is what protects against subtle harm, because only at that size can a 0.1-point rise be seen. Dwell times cover a business day (the daily cycle) and then a weekend; some defects appear only at scale or over hours.</p>\n<p><strong>What the ladder cannot see.</strong> A change in conversion of a fraction of a percent needs far more users than a rollout reaches in a few days, so a rollout watches for harm, and the question of benefit is for an experiment with a holdout (C14).</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Stage sizes follow from the smallest harm you will not accept, and the arithmetic shows why a 1% canary is not the same as safety against every problem.</p>\n<span id=\"c17-worked-example-2-launch-or-hold-with-mixed-results-illustrat\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 2 — Launch or hold with mixed results (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a streaming service of the kind Netflix operates. No fact about any real product is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The readout.</strong> A test of a new recommendation approach ran one week on 10,000,000 members (invented). The rule, fixed beforehand (FW-C14-05): launch if the primary interval's lower end is at or above the threshold and all guardrails are within margins.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Metric</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n<th>95% interval</th>\n<th>Rule</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Viewing hours per member (primary)</td>\n<td>+1.5%</td>\n<td>+0.3% to +2.7%</td>\n<td>Threshold +1.0%: lower end below it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>30-day retention (guardrail)</td>\n<td>−0.2 points</td>\n<td>−0.8 to +0.4</td>\n<td>Margin −0.5: lower end breaches</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Support contacts (guardrail)</td>\n<td>0%</td>\n<td>−2% to +2%</td>\n<td>Margin +3%: within</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Apply the order (FW-C14-07, FW-C17-04).</strong> Validity passes. Guardrails: retention's lower bound (−0.8) is outside the margin, but the interval includes zero and positive values; it is uncertainty, not evidence of harm. Primary: the estimate (+1.5%) is above the threshold, and the interval straddles it. By the strict rule: do not launch as is; extend.</p>\n<p><strong>What would extending take?</strong> The retention interval has a width of 1.2 points. Halving it to ±0.3 needs four times the data. If a week gave this, four weeks in total, so three more. During those three weeks, the test could run at a larger exposure: that is itself a staged launch, with a holdout, and the guardrail watched daily against the margin.</p>\n<p><strong>Cost of delay.</strong> If the true gain is +1.5% in viewing, and it is worth, say, $4,000,000 a year across the member base (invented), three weeks of delay costs about $230,000. Against the risk of a lasting retention loss: a 0.5-point fall in 30-day retention across 10,000,000 members is 50,000 members, at a value of, say, $100 each, $5,000,000. The cost of waiting is small beside the downside, which is why the extension is justified.</p>\n<p><strong>Novelty check.</strong> A week is also too short to rule out novelty (C14.4). The extension helps with that too: the effect by week should settle.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Stage it: expand to 25% of members with a 5% holdout for three more weeks; launch fully if the primary lower bound clears +1.0% and retention's lower bound is above −0.5; otherwise do not launch or redesign. Tell the team the rule and the dates now.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The decision followed a rule written first, the arithmetic of delay against downside, and a partial option that turned &quot;wait&quot; into &quot;learn while limiting exposure.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c17-worked-example-3-a-defect-two-weeks-before-manufacturing-loc\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 3 — A defect two weeks before manufacturing locks (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a wireless accessory of the kind Apple and several other companies make. No fact about any real product is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> An accessory will be manufactured in a first run of 500,000 units. Manufacturing locks in 14 days: after that, changes to the device require a delay of weeks and cost millions. Testing finds a defect in the device's firmware: in 4% of units in lab tests (invented), the accessory loses its connection to the phone intermittently. Firmware can be updated over the air if the device can connect. A delay of the lock by 14 days would, the team estimates, miss the holiday sales window and cost $3,000,000 of margin (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Classify (FW-C17-03).</strong> The manufactured part is irreversible; the firmware is changeable only through the update path. So the question is whether the fix-later path is real.</p>\n<p><strong>The circular risk.</strong> The update needs a working connection. The defect is a connection loss. If the affected units cannot reliably receive the update, &quot;fix it later in software&quot; fails exactly when it is needed. So the first test is not &quot;can we write a fix&quot; but &quot;can an affected unit receive it.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Options and numbers.</strong> 4% of 500,000 is 20,000 units. A field problem costs about $60 per affected unit (returns, support, replacement; invented).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>A: Lock on time with a gate.</em> By day 7, test the update path on 200 affected units. Suppose a 0.8 chance it works on at least 90% of affected units. If it works, residual defects are 10% of 20,000, 2,000 units, costing $120,000. If it fails (0.2), delay the lock by the remaining 7 days at a cost of $1,500,000 (half the full delay, since the schedule has absorbed a week). Expected cost: 0.8 × $120,000 + 0.2 × $1,500,000 = $96,000 + $300,000 = $396,000.</li>\n<li><em>B: Delay the lock 14 days now.</em> Cost $3,000,000 for sure.</li>\n<li><em>C: Lock on time and ship as is.</em> 20,000 × $60 = $1,200,000, plus a reputational cost.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On expected cost A ($396,000) beats C ($1,200,000) and B ($3,000,000). The value of A is the gate: it keeps the option to delay while gathering the one piece of evidence that decides.</p>\n<p><strong>Supply and the rest.</strong> Check also whether the first run is sized for a launch with possible returns, whether units from the first weeks can be segregated and updated before shipping, and whether the update can be pushed from day one so most customers get the fix on first connection. Brief support on the symptom and the workaround. Certification: confirm that a firmware change does not require repeating approvals <code>[V: FCC equipment authorization, 47 CFR Part 2 and section 2.1043 on firmware (permissive changes); US rules for radio devices only; safety approvals and non-US regimes not checked]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Choose A. Run the update-path test by day 7 on the affected units, with a stated criterion (updates succeed on at least 90% of affected units), and decide on day 7 whether to lock or to delay. If the defect were a safety risk, the options table would change: safety is not traded against cost.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> For an irreversible step, the work is to create an early test that decides, so that the choice to lock or delay is made with evidence while it is still possible to delay.</p>\n<span id=\"c17-worked-example-4-rollback-triggers-that-respect-noise-illust\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 4 — Rollback triggers that respect noise (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A checkout redesign launches to 10% of users. Payment success is the key metric, with a baseline of 96% (invented) and about 1,000 attempts per half hour in the exposed group.</p>\n<p><strong>Noise first (FW-C17-05).</strong> Over a half hour, the standard error of the success rate is √(0.96 × 0.04 ÷ 1,000) = 0.62 points. A drop of 1.0 point is only 1.6 standard errors: the chance of seeing a drop that large by luck in a given window is about 5%. With 48 half-hour windows a day, that is about 2.6 false alarms a day. A trigger of &quot;success below 95% in any half hour&quot; would fire by chance almost every few hours and teach people to ignore it.</p>\n<p>Over two hours, with 4,000 attempts, the standard error is 0.31 points, and a 1.0-point drop is 3.2 standard errors: a chance of about 0.06% per window, with 12 windows a day, under 1% of days with a false alarm. So the slow trigger is reliable and the fast one is not.</p>\n<p><strong>The trigger sheet.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Metric</th>\n<th>Threshold</th>\n<th>Window</th>\n<th>Owner</th>\n<th>Action</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Payment success (exposed users)</td>\n<td>Below 95.0%</td>\n<td>Rolling 2 hours</td>\n<td>On-call engineer</td>\n<td>Turn off the flag; page the PM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Payment success</td>\n<td>Below 92.0%</td>\n<td>Any 15 minutes</td>\n<td>On-call engineer</td>\n<td>Turn off the flag immediately</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Checkout error rate</td>\n<td>Above 2.5%</td>\n<td>Rolling 1 hour</td>\n<td>On-call engineer</td>\n<td>Turn off the flag</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Support contacts about payment</td>\n<td>Above 1.5× baseline</td>\n<td>Rolling 4 hours</td>\n<td>Support lead, then PM</td>\n<td>Pause the ramp; review</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Any payment of the wrong amount</td>\n<td>One confirmed case</td>\n<td>Immediate</td>\n<td>PM and engineering lead</td>\n<td>Turn off the flag; incident</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Why two thresholds for one metric.</strong> A large, fast drop (below 92% in 15 minutes) is clear even in a small window because the effect is far larger than the noise; a smaller, slower drop needs the longer window. Together they cover both severe and subtle failures. The last trigger is a single event, not a rate: some failures (money wrong) are unacceptable in any number.</p>\n<p><strong>What the PM does.</strong> Agree the sheet with engineering and support before the launch, put it where the on-call can see it, and agree that anyone on-call can act on a hard trigger without asking.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Triggers set without noise either cry wolf or sleep through the fire, and settled in advance they remove a debate from the worst moment.</p>\n<span id=\"c17-worked-example-5-delivery-risk-compounds-illustrative\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 5 — Delivery risk compounds (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A launch depends on five things from outside the core team (invented): legal sign-off, a partner's API, design assets, a security review and localization.</p>\n<p><strong>The map (FW-C17-07).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dependency</th>\n<th>P(on time)</th>\n<th>Impact if late</th>\n<th>Mitigation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Legal sign-off</td>\n<td>90%</td>\n<td>Blocks launch</td>\n<td>Submit the brief now; weekly check</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Partner API</td>\n<td>85%</td>\n<td>Blocks the main feature</td>\n<td>Build against a mock; agree a fallback</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Design assets</td>\n<td>95%</td>\n<td>Delays polish</td>\n<td>Ship with placeholders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security review</td>\n<td>90%</td>\n<td>Blocks launch</td>\n<td>Book the slot now</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Localization</td>\n<td>92%</td>\n<td>Delays some markets</td>\n<td>Launch in the first market</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>The compounding.</strong> The chance that all five arrive on time is 0.90 × 0.85 × 0.95 × 0.90 × 0.92 = 0.60. A plan that reads as five &quot;probably fine&quot; items has a 40% chance of slipping.</p>\n<p><strong>What to do.</strong> Raise the weak ones: building against a mock and agreeing a fallback lifts the partner API item to 93%; booking the security review now lifts it to 95%. The new product: 0.90 × 0.93 × 0.95 × 0.95 × 0.92 = 0.70. Reduce the count on the critical path: launch in the first market so localization does not block. Add buffer where risks cluster (legal and security together: allow a week). Start the longest-lead items first.</p>\n<p><strong>Scope cuts, in advance.</strong> If the date is threatened, cut in this order: placeholders instead of final assets; fewer markets at launch; a smaller first feature set. Never cut the launch criteria (quality bar, accessibility, security).</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate early.</strong> Review the map weekly. A dependency that is slipping is raised that week with options, not on the day it fails.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The probability arithmetic converted vague worry into a 60% number, and each mitigation had a measurable effect on it.</p>\n<span id=\"c17-6-baseline-strong-standout\"></span>\n<h3>6. Baseline, Strong, Standout</h3>\n<p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;You are launching a redesigned checkout in six weeks. Walk me through it.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c17-baseline-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Baseline answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I'd make sure engineering finishes on time, do testing, and then release it to everyone with a marketing push. If something goes wrong, we'd fix it.&quot;</p>\n";
